\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Prediction-powered inference.}
PPI builds a valid interval for a mean, quantile or regression coefficient from a few gold-standard labels, many unlabeled records and a black-box predictor \citep{angelopoulos2023ppi}; PPI++ tunes a scalar weight $\lambda$ on the predictor and is the strongest published interval for the fixed-predictor setting we study \citep{angelopoulos2023ppipp}. Stratified PPI \citep{fisch2024stratified} recalibrates the predictor on strata, which is the closest published relative of using labels to build the control variate (our calibrated class contains control variates that are constant on strata of $f$, but we do not run stratified PPI as published), and a bootstrap version of PPI avoids the asymptotic variance estimate \citep{zrnic2024note}. Cross-PPI \citep{zrnic2024cross} trains the predictor on the same labels, and active inference \citep{zrnic2024active} chooses which records to label; both change what the setting holds fixed, so neither is a comparator here (with uniform labeling, active inference reduces to PPI++). Earlier post-prediction methods model the relation between predicted and observed outcomes on the labeled sample \citep{wang2020methods,miao2023assumption}. Our baseline is the \texttt{ppi-python} implementation of PPI++, and we show that its clip of the estimated weight matters on two datasets.

\paragraph{Semi-supervised efficiency, missing data and surrogates.}
That the best control variate is the regression function $\E[Y\mid Z]$ is the classical efficiency principle for missing data \citep{robins1994estimation,tsiatis2006semiparametric} and for semi-supervised mean estimation \citep{zhang2019semi,zhang2022high}; the same line treats regression coefficients and general M-estimands \citep{chakrabortty2018efficient,azriel2022semi,song2024general}. Cross-fitted influence-function estimators are double machine learning \citep{chernozhukov2018double}, a doubly robust estimator in the sense of \citet{bang2005doubly}. Close relatives use labels to recalibrate a score \citep{gronsbell2022efficient} or treat predictions as surrogates \citep{kallus2024surrogates}. With a linear control variate the pooled estimator is the regression estimator of survey sampling \citep{cochran1977sampling}. We claim none of these principles. We write the exact gap between PPI++ and the floor, measure it on the standard PPI datasets, and test when learning the control variate pays.

\paragraph{Interval constructions.}
Wald intervals undercover for proportions at small $n$; Wilson-type and studentised bootstrap intervals are the usual repairs \citep{wilson1927probable,brown2001interval,efron1993introduction}.
